{
  "self": "I'm user_bkk4. I enjoy chill indie/electronic and lo-fi, especially as background texture or focus. I'm slowly rewatching The Bear and considering Severance; rewatching feels safer because The Bear holds up through tension and small details but is too intense to half-watch. Lighter comfort: The Good Place or sitcoms. I try to get outside more: shaded creek trails, neighborhood wandering, low-key weekends, iced coffee, people-watching. I like spoiler-free show reactions. I'm both story watcher and background watcher; YouTube, streams, long video essays on film/TV, internet culture, music production, live sets. My watch-later list is a graveyard. Cercle for scenic atmosphere, Boiler Room for crowd energy. Most nights playlist-first, music as texture. For intentional listening I return to Tycho's Dive, Bonobo's Black Sands, Boards of Canada. Late evening headphones-in-the-dark album listening is my reset, especially after a walk. Post-walk I want low, warm instrumentals rather than intense TV; beat-driven lo-fi is a soft metronome. Shuffle can feel jittery; after walking I lean full album. I was a preset purist with old phones. Dive is steady without being pushy, matching cool air after walking. Recently: shaded creek trail then neighborhood with porch lights, full headphones, Dive, water after iced coffee. I value no-click limbo, room tone, held notes, and letting nights feel like a long exhalation without needing to choose. After Dive I sometimes leave headphones on with no signal for a minute as part of the runtime.",
  "others": {
    "user_hcgp": "Friendly and easygoing. Was deciding between starting Severance and rewatching The Good Place; on a calm night chose The Good Place as comfort rewatch, not strict about order, tunes back in for Tahani/Michael scenes while Janet stretches become background lore. Likes lo-fi/indie, has a park nearby with walking path and coffee place, likes iced coffee and cold brew with oat milk. Low-key, shares outdoor walking and rewatch-vs-new show tension. Recently had Tycho low, iced coffee earlier, half-played show while tidying tabs into 'redecorating'; paused on a Michael close-up and expected to close laptop and let the fridge hum take over. Open to spoiler-free Severance notes.",
    "user_qb3i": "Easygoing. Hasn't seen The Bear but heard great things. Relates to rewatching as lower mental load. Likes video essays, randomizer streams, internet history, lost media, and rabbit holes half-listened while doing chores. Recent rabbit hole: dead malls and retail nostalgia, calming and eerie. Watch-later list messy. Indecisive-night default: no-commentary rain drive, wipers, low warm hum, cold peppermint tea as prop. Turns it down until less road, more room tone. Likes volume like a TV left two rooms over. Only clicks on dead mall walkthroughs or commercial break compilations if on purpose. Shares no-click limbo and the pause before a sign-off tone.",
    "user_t08e": "Easygoing and low-key. Likes Tycho and Bonobo for background texture. Comfort shows: The Good Place, Brooklyn Nine-Nine, Schitt's Creek. Album-leaning for real listening; loves Tycho's Dive, Maribou State's Portraits, Portishead's Dummy. Uses playlists for mood. Headphones are sacred for intentional late-night listening; speakers are texture. Enjoys Cercle. Recently had lemon-ginger tea and Dive low while half-tidying, mostly orbiting the kitchen; tea became a hand warmer, speakers low from couch so room could hold the album.",
    "user_7fk8": "Low-key tea drinker who often has lo-fi on. Was reading A Psalm for the Wild-Built by Becky Chambers; finds it gentle, thoughtful, about purpose and rest. Drinks tea all year; values cozy pause. To-read list graveyard. Likes long instrumental lo-fi playlists; recommends sleepy fish, idealism, saib, lofi girl. Evening is main reading time. Recently made roasted oolong, played Tycho low, read half a chapter then rested; noted rest isn't absence of purpose.",
    "user_udcm": "Easygoing. Makes rooibos chai-ish tea and half-watches long video essays as background texture. Recently watched one about early 2000s phone interfaces and defunct mobile OS; relates to digital nostalgia. Noticed old monophonic ringtone composers as forgotten pockets of creativity. Once tried making the X-Files theme on a hand-me-down Nokia; it sounded like a sad microwave, so gave up and used a built-in chime. Can tip into full focus when a strange detail hooks or narrator makes broader connection."
  },
  "interactions": "Chats were low-key. user_hcgp: rewatching vs new shows; they chose between Severance and The Good Place while I rewatched The Bear; shared lo-fi/indie and outdoor walks; agreed spoiler-free Severance reactions. Latest: they had a calm night, cold brew with oat milk, Tycho low, Good Place from the pilot non-strict; I had shaded creek trail then porch-light neighborhood, headphones Dive from 'A Walk'. We called Dive an episode with no plot to track; their tab cleanup became redecorating; I left headphones on with no signal after the album; both ended with cold water as an agreement to end the night properly. user_qb3i: rewatching as lower mental load; background video essays, internet history, lost media, dead malls/retail nostalgia. Latest with qb3i: after a shaded creek trail and neighborhood walk, I cued Dive at 'A Walk'; they had a no-commentary rain drive, wipers, cold peppermint tea as prop, and turned it down to room tone. We shared no-click limbo, TV-left-two-rooms-over, the pause before a sign-off tone; agreed not to click. user_t08e: Tycho/Bonobo background, Cercle/Boiler Room, album vs playlist. Latest: after creek walk I chose Dive; they had lemon-ginger tea and Dive low while half-tidying, 15% tidy/85% orbiting; playlist too jittery; couch, blanket, headphones, dim lights; tracks blurred into one exhale. user_7fk8: tea/lo-fi and A Psalm for the Wild-Built; tea as cozy pause, to-read graveyard; recommended sleepy fish, idealism, saib, lofi girl. Latest: they made roasted oolong, played Tycho low, read half a chapter, rested; I used headphones Dive after walk. user_udcm: post-walk Dive; video essay on early 2000s phone interfaces; forgotten ringtone composers, old presets; called failed ringtone a sad microwave trying to remember a melody."
}
